  "worked_solution": "Here, we use the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${cos}^{2}\u2061x$ term, given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}\u20612x=2{cos}^{2}\u2061x-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n2{cos}^{2}\u2061x=\\operatorname{cos}\u20612x+1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $3,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n6{cos}^{2}\u2061x=3(\\operatorname{cos}\u20612x+1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }6{cos}^{2}\u2061x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }3(\\operatorname{cos}\u20612x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\u20612x+3x)|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =[\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2\\pi )+3\\pi ]-[\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\pi )+3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}] \\\\  & =3\\pi -\\frac{3\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
